\title{A short proof when the subspaces share a coordinate basis}
\taxon{Lemma}
\meta{status}{proof-account}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Elementary special case of [OpenAI](cn-0042), Lemma 2.1, written on 12 October 2026 as a warm-up for the comparison with [Scott and Wilmer](cn-0072). The counting argument is supplied here.}

\p{\strong{Statement.} Let #{m\geq1} and let #{e_1,\ldots,e_N} be a basis of a finite-dimensional vector space #{W} over any field. Suppose each #{U_i} and #{V_i}, #{1\leq i\leq m}, is spanned by a subset of this same basis, and #{U_i\cap V_i=0}. Then}

##{\sum_{i,j=1}^{m}\dim(U_i\cap V_j)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m^2}{4}\right\rfloor N.}

\p{\strong{Proof.} For each basis index #{k}, let #{a_k} be the number of spaces #{U_i} containing #{e_k}, and #{b_k} the number of spaces #{V_i} containing #{e_k}. The two sets of indices are disjoint, since no designated pair contains a common basis vector. Hence #{a_k+b_k\leq m}, so #{a_kb_k\leq\lfloor m^2/4\rfloor}.}

\p{The intersection of two spaces spanned by subsets of one basis is spanned by their common basis vectors. Count the contribution of each #{e_k} to the sum: it occurs in exactly #{a_kb_k} ordered intersections. Therefore}

##{\sum_{i,j=1}^{m}\dim(U_i\cap V_j)=\sum_{k=1}^{N}a_kb_k\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m^2}{4}\right\rfloor N.}

\p{This explains the balanced product in [the general bound](cn-0024) and [its equality construction](cn-0025). Arbitrary subspace families need not admit one basis of this kind; [the exterior-algebra comparison](cn-0072) explains why taking leading coordinates does not remove that restriction.}
